To determine if a number is a power of three in Java without using loops or recursion, you can utilize the bitwise shift operator and the concept of logarithms (though without explicitly computing logs). Here's a concise and efficient approach:

```java
public class Main {
    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // Check for the base case: n == 0
        if (n == 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // Perform bitwise shifts to check for powers of 3
        while (n != 0) {
            n >>= 1;  // Equivalent to n /= 3
            if (n == 0) {
                return true;
            }
        }

        // If we've made it here, n is not a power of 3
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test cases
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(27));  // Output: true
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(0));    // Output: false
        System.out.println(isPowerOfThree(-1));   // Output: false
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

This function uses bitwise right shifting (`>>=`)

